  "query_preview": "..Today...Following the passage of a weak upper-level \ntrough yesterday, brief ridging with much drier air has taken \nhold today. Precipitation chances drop to zero across all of \nnorthern Arizona as a result. Expect a pleasant day with south to \nsouthwest afternoon breezes and near normal daytime temperatures.\n\nSunday through Tuesday...We are setting up to have an active stretch \nof weather to begin the week. A pair of tropical systems in the \nPacific (Hurricane Polo and Hurricane Odalys) are forecast to drift \nnorth through the period. Meanwhile, a deep west coast trough is \nforecast to dive southeastward along the California coast on Sunday-\nMonday, then eventually cross directly over Arizona. While the \nremnant tropical circulations will not directly impact northern \nArizona, the trough will interact with the enhanced field of moisture\naround the systems and we will see a deep surge of moisture into \nthe region.\n\nUpper-level moisture looks to arrive as early as Sunday morning as a \nsubtle shortwave ripples ahead of the deeper west coast low. We may \nbegin the day with some high-based activity, but will quickly \nmoisten up in the lower levels by Sunday evening. Precipitation \nchances ramp up Sunday night, becoming widespread by Monday as \nmoisture continues to stream in ahead of the approaching trough. \nGiven the setup, there is risk of heavy rainfall and flash flooding \nthrough the period. The latest WPC ERO puts all of northern Arizona \nin the Slight risk category on Monday. Also, enhanced shear from the \ntrough could allow for better storm organization and some threat of \nsevere storms. There still remains a degree of uncertainty among \nensemble guidance on how progressive the trough will be, however \nconfidence is increasing towards a slower set up that will bring \nanother active day on Tuesday with more flooding threats and severe \nthreats. Besides precipitation, expect breezy south-southwest winds \non Sunday and Monday and cool daytime temperatures to start the week.\n\nWednesday through Friday...By Wednesday, the trough axis will be \npushing out of our area. Precipitation chances will decline from \nwest to east as much drier air and subsidence follows in its wake. \nExpect dry conditions with warming daytime temperatures to end the \nweek.",
